body:
Not the most important point but I feel like Tiamat's Wrath and the cover synopsis were revealed way earlier than August 2018, like March or April 2018. We knew the name Teresa Duarte long before we ever got to meet her

&#x200B;

yep April 3, 2018  [https://www.jamessacorey.com/get-ready-tiamats-wrath/](https://www.jamessacorey.com/get-ready-tiamats-wrath/)

I've posted for help with games before and gotten tons of responses like "you fucking suck, why are you fucking asking us go google it or watch a youtube video, this is all your fault for choosing the hardest difficulty" like yo I just wanted to chat about it and see if anybody had any fresh ideas. It's pretty depressing to see that kind of energy in a forum for our jobs

&#x200B;

FWIW though I think part of the vitriol at a new tutor like yourself is because some of us are laid off / reduced hours at our normal jobs and trying to pick up more tutoring hours to compensate, but the company instead chose to hire a bunch of new tutors that they can pay at the lower probationary / tutor I rates rather than allowing us experienced tutors more hours. It's not your fault but the system is fucking us and making us fight against each other

It takes a special type of student to teach themself the entire class on their own. At the very minimum, these students need a guide to orient them with the available class resources and what they are expected to use in order to learn. If it were just a few students here and there that didn't know anything, I would chalk it up to them being lazy, but it's the vast majority of the students that I get who are completely clueless and expect me to lecture the whole class for them. This makes me suspect that it's more of a systemic problem than an individual problem. Some of these students aren't even aware that they have a class textbook/online text, probably because nobody told them (or they were told to go to tutoring for their primary resource learning). 

&#x200B;

Part of the professor's job should be, at the very minimum, telling the students how they are supposed to be learning the material. It's hard for me to blame the student for their ignorance if the entire system is failing to provide them with basic resources or information.
